      "commit": "fa7bec130b4864996a51fe25aa49c7dcf08f614d",
      "source_file": "src/sqllens/tools/query_database.py",
      "claim_text": "_rollback_conversation guards the conversation store's missing truncate_messages method but not a call to it that raises; its only caller is the except asyncio.CancelledError handler, so an exception would replace the propagating cancellation with nothing recording that the rollback did not happen.",
      "evidence": "Confirmed at source: the getattr lookup was guarded but the call itself was not, and the caller re-raises the cancellation immediately after. Wrapped the call in except Exception -> logger.exception naming the store + return, matching the sibling missing-method branch and CLAUDE.md's structured-signal rule. Added tests/unit/test_query_database_queue.py::test_rollback_whose_truncate_raises_is_logged_and_does_not_escape pinning the ERROR log and that the store's error does not escape. Docstring updated so the claim matches the two guards now present."
    },
    {
      "finding_id": "VC-17",
      "decision": "applied",
      "commit": "fa7bec130b4864996a51fe25aa49c7dcf08f614d",
      "source_file": "docs/internal/database-connectors/cleanup-error-handling.md",
      "claim_text": "The sentence 'The cleanup in the finally: above still runs on that same thread, before the exception surfaces at the await' is false in the cancellation case the paragraph is about: asyncio.to_thread cannot interrupt a running worker, so CancelledError surfaces at the await immediately while the thread's finally cleanup runs later.",
      "evidence": "Applied the fix discipline's prose order: deleted the attacked false ordering clause, adding nothing, keeping the true thread-affinity half ('still runs on that same thread'). Acceptance criterion 11 only requires this file to state that a cancelled request's query keeps running on its worker thread until it finishes or the statement timeout stops it, which the untouched bolded sentence earlier in the same item states, so the criterion still holds without the deleted clause."
    },
    {
      "finding_id": "F-4",
      "decision": "applied",
      "commit": "fa7bec130b4864996a51fe25aa49c7dcf08f614d",
      "source_file": "docs/internal/database-connectors/cleanup-error-handling.md",
      "claim_text": "The added sentence that the cleanup in the finally: block still runs on that same thread before the exception surfaces at the await holds for an exception raised inside the threaded body but not for the cancellation case the surrounding paragraph is about.",
      "evidence": "Same site and same defect as checklist FAIL VC-17, which the engine return itself notes; resolved by the same single prose deletion."
    },
    {
      "finding_id": "F-5",
      "decision": "applied",
      "commit": "fa7bec130b4864996a51fe25aa49c7dcf08f614d",
      "source_file": "src/sqllens/agent/integrations/sqlite/sql_runner.py",
      "claim_text": "The inline comment repeats the same ordering claim, that teardown runs on the thread that owns the connection, before any exception surfaces at the await. The thread-affinity half is correct; the ordering half is wrong under cancellation.",
      "evidence": "Class sibling of VC-17, found by searching the repo for the ordering phrasing (Grep over 'before any exception surfaces' / 'surfaces at the await' / 'runs on that same thread' returned exactly these two sites). Fixed by the same deletion of the false ordering clause, keeping the load-bearing thread-affinity half. The MySQL and Postgres runners carry no equivalent sentence."
    },
    {
      "finding_id": "D-1",
      "decision": "applied",
      "commit": "f0ab0d31db0a5949d5e210d1d3e278be25d948c6",
      "source_file": "tests/unit/test_query_database_queue.py",
      "claim_text": "The new test's only breadcrumb assertion is 'was not rolled back' plus the store class name, substrings shared verbatim with the sibling missing-method arm's log line, so it never keys on the arm-specific 'raised from truncate_messages' text and would still pass if the raising-store case were reported through the wrong arm's breadcrumb.",
      "original_severity": "minor",
      "evidence": "Valid: the two arms share the 'was not rolled back' prefix and both name the store class, so the assertion did not distinguish them. Corrected like an item-3 sibling by also requiring the arm-specific 'raised from truncate_messages' substring; the re-gate confirmed the test cannot pass via the missing-method arm and changes state when the guard is removed. Inner attempt 1 of 2."
    }
  ],
